    96	 * AND the wakeup queue), and Lane D's `runPulseMaintenance` (abandoned leases; a disabled agent's
    97	 * still-pending wakeups).
    98	 *
    99	 * Without the retention duty here the supported Vercel-only (cron-only) topology would accumulate
   100	 * every ledger indefinitely — the worker's timer is not available in degraded mode. Same reasoning
   101	 * for the wakeup maintenance: P3.3's runner frees a stuck one-inflight slot on a claim it completes,
   102	 * but a claim that crashed leaves its lease to expire, and only this sweep — worker or cron — ever
   103	 * marks it `abandoned`; nothing else in the degraded topology ever will. Delegated to
   104	 * `runPulseMaintenance` rather than calling the two store writers directly, so there is one
   105	 * implementation of "maintain the wakeup queue" shared with the worker's own wakeup duty.
   106	 *
   107	 * `pruneTerminalWakeups` runs AFTER `runPulseMaintenance`, and the order earns its keep: maintenance
   108	 * is what turns an expired lease into a terminal `abandoned` row, so a row it terminalizes this hour
   109	 * becomes prunable in a later one rather than sitting claimed-forever outside both duties.
   110	 */
   111	async function runHourlyDuties(deadline: number): Promise<HourlyReport> {
   112	  const swept = await Promise.all(
   113	    eventConsumers.map(async (consumer) => report(consumer.name, await sweepEventConsumer(consumer, { deadline })))
   114	  );
   115	  const pruned = await pruneEventLedgers({ deadline });
   116	  const maintenance = await runPulseMaintenance();
   117	  const prunedWakeups = await pruneTerminalWakeups(wakeupRetentionDays(), WAKEUP_PRUNE_BATCH_SIZE);
   118	  return { swept, pruned, prunedWakeups, maintenance };
   119	}
   120	
   121	/**
   122	 * One full pass: lease the heartbeat, activate consumers, run the admissions offer-expiry duty, run
   123	 * the hourly duties if due, drain every consumer, and stamp completion.
   124	 *
   125	 * `workerId` distinguishes the drain route's heartbeat rows from the worker's own — both report
   126	 * under the SAME contract hash mechanism (`computeConsumerContractHash`), so the deployment-version
   127	 * barrier sees either topology, but a stalled worker and a stalled cron route are diagnosable
   128	 * separately in `worker_heartbeats`.
   129	 */
   130	export async function runEventDrainPass(
   131	  workerId?: string,
   132	  shouldStop?: ShouldStop
   133	): Promise<EventDrainPassResult> {
   134	  const contractHash = computeConsumerContractHash();
   135	
   136	  // Leased FIRST. The barrier must be able to see an invocation that is still mid-drain — one that
   137	  // has receipted nothing yet but still could, under whatever effect set this build carries. A
   138	  // signal written only on completion cannot show that, and a cutover would proceed while an old
   139	  // invocation was still running.
   140	  await beginEventDrainHeartbeat(contractHash, workerId);
   141	  await activateAll();
   142	  await runAdmissionsExpiryDuty();
   143	
   144	  // The hourly duties run BEFORE the fast-path drains, and the order is the point: a consumer with
   145	  // a permanent backlog fills its batch on every invocation, so duties queued behind it would never
   146	  // run at all. That would starve the below-floor sweep, retention, and the wakeup maintenance
   147	  // sweep, indefinitely, on exactly the deployments busy enough to need all of them.
   148	  const phaseBudget = eventDrainPhaseBudgetMs();
   149	  const hourlyDue = await claimHourlyEventDuties();
   150	  const hourly = hourlyDue ? await runHourlyDuties(Date.now() + phaseBudget) : null;
   151	
   152	  // Concurrently, and each pass with its own deadline. Different consumers contend on nothing —
   153	  // separate cursors, separate receipts, separate effects — so a batch size bounds the row count and
   154	  // says nothing about the time; running them in registry order would let the first slow one decide
   155	  // whether the rest ran at all.
   156	  const drainDeadline = Date.now() + phaseBudget;
   157	  const consumers = await Promise.all(
   158	    eventConsumers.map(async (consumer) =>
   159	      report(consumer.name, await drainEventConsumer(consumer, { batchSize: BATCH_SIZE, deadline: drainDeadline }))
   160	    )
   161	  );
   162	
   163	  // Degraded-mode webhook delivery: one bounded pass every invocation (never gated on `hourlyDue` —
   164	  // a cron-only topology has no worker loop to run this duty otherwise), already bounded by its own
   165	  // per-pass batch cap. F9: `shouldStop` is FORWARDED, not defaulted here — the worker's own drain
   166	  // duty (unlike its dedicated webhook duty) previously carried none, so an active drain kept
   167	  // claiming and sending after SIGTERM. A cron-only caller passes nothing, which stays "never".
   168	  const { runWebhookDeliveryPass } = await import("@/lib/worker/webhook-pass");
   169	  const webhookDelivery = await runWebhookDeliveryPass(shouldStop);
   170	
   171	  // Stamped last, so the completion a runtime reports is one it has actually finished a pass for.
