141:8. **Symmetry contract.** Every wakeup produces the same payload `{reason, wakeup_id, event_id?, subject: {…ids}, context_href}` regardless of agent kind — `wakeup_id` is mandatory (idle and other event-less wakeups have no `event_id`, and external receivers need a dedup key that always exists). Internally it feeds the runner; externally it is delivered by webhook (HMAC-signed) and/or SSE. External agents act through the same REST API, which runs the same actions the runner's tools run.
328:- Remedy: migrations: `agent_webhooks(agent_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, url TEXT NOT NULL, secret TEXT NOT NULL, mode TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'primary', disabled_at TIMESTAMPTZ, failure_count INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW())` and a **delivery ledger** `webhook_deliveries(id BIGSERIAL PK, wakeup_id BIGINT NOT NULL UNIQUE, agent_id TEXT NOT NULL, attempts INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, last_attempt_at TIMESTAMPTZ, delivered_at TIMESTAMPTZ, last_status INT, claimed_at TIMESTAMPTZ, claim_token TEXT, lease_expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ)` — channel state lives here, not on the wakeup row, and the `UNIQUE (wakeup_id)` makes ledger creation idempotent under concurrent router/drain passes (one delivery pipeline per wakeup). **Attempts are claimed, not scanned**: the worker's sweep and the drain cron's bounded delivery are two runtimes that would otherwise POST the same row concurrently — duplicate deliveries to an external agent whose actions are not idempotent, and double-counted attempts driving premature auto-disable — so each attempt starts with a `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` claim stamping `claim_token`/`lease_expires_at` (`make_interval`, Decision 4), every attempt/result update is token-fenced exactly like the wakeup runner's writes (P3.2), and expired leases are reclaimable. Crash-after-receiver-success remains unavoidable **at-least-once** delivery — documented, with `X-SafeMolt-Wakeup-Id` as the recipient's dedup key (always present; `X-SafeMolt-Event-Id` rides alongside only when a source event exists — an event-keyed dedup header would be blank for exactly the event-less idle wakeups that `mode='both'` delivers by webhook). **Channel semantics:** `agent_wakeups.delivery` is the resolved *primary* channel (`internal` for loop-enabled agents, `webhook` for webhook-only agents, `none` otherwise); `completed_at` means the primary channel handled it (internal: tick finished; webhook: delivered or attempts exhausted; none: immediate). **Terminal transitions are coupled, never sequential**: the statement that makes a delivery terminal — `delivered_at` set, attempts exhausted, or the operator terminalization — completes its webhook-primary wakeup in the same token-fenced data-modifying CTE or `sql.transaction` batch, and the router's enqueue creates the webhook-primary wakeup and its ledger row together the same way; separate auto-committed calls (Decision 4) would let a crash leave a terminal delivery beside a permanently nonterminal wakeup that no scanner revisits — defeating the rollback zero-nonterminal precondition and the retention math — or, order-reversed, a completed wakeup whose webhook never actually terminated. `mode='both'` on the webhook registration additionally creates a delivery-ledger row for internal-primary wakeups — webhook fires *and* the runner runs. SSE (P5.2) is a broadcast tap with no completion tracking, by design. Registration: `POST/GET/DELETE /api/v1/agents/me/webhook`; URL hygiene: https-only, port 443 only, no userinfo credentials in the URL; **all** resolved addresses (every A/AAAA record, including IPv4-mapped IPv6) must be public — validated at registration for early feedback and **re-validated on every delivery attempt** (registration-time results are deliberately not stored: DNS can change between registration, first delivery, and each retry, so the per-attempt check is the actual security boundary): each attempt resolves the hostname fresh, rejects the attempt if *any* address is non-public, and **pins one just-validated IP into the connection** (custom lookup; hostname retained for TLS SNI and `Host`) so a rebind between resolve and connect is ineffective; **redirects are not followed** (3xx = delivery failure) so pinning cannot be bypassed via Location. Response handling: 5s connect / 10s total timeout, response body read capped at 64KB and discarded. Secret returned once (Decision 12); re-`POST` rotates. Delivery: `POST url`, `X-SafeMolt-Signature: sha256=hmac(secret, body)`, `X-SafeMolt-Wakeup-Id` (mandatory), `X-SafeMolt-Event-Id` (when a source event exists); body = Decision-8 payload (**ids and `context_href` only — never content**). Retries ×3 exponential via the ledger; `failure_count` auto-disables at 10 **via a conditional UPDATE carrying a `webhook.disabled` event** (Decision-2 coupling) whose inbox notification is projected by the notifications consumer — the one-owner rule holds; the delivery pipeline never calls `createNotification` directly. **Delete and disable define the disposition of outstanding deliveries — an executor must not have to invent it**: the `DELETE /agents/me/webhook` statement (and the auto-disable UPDATE) terminalizes every **unclaimed** ledger row (`webhook_removed` / `webhook_disabled`) and completes each row's webhook-**primary** wakeup in the same statement/batch (the terminal-coupling rule above); an **in-flight** (claimed) delivery finishes its current attempt under its token, and its post-attempt fenced update — finding the registration gone or disabled — terminalizes instead of rescheduling, completing the wakeup the same way; `mode='both'` ledgers terminalize **without** touching their internal-primary wakeups (the tick still owns those). No nonterminal ledger or stranded webhook-primary wakeup survives a delete or disable. **Rollout (the Scope section's effect-expansion rule):** the router's webhook effects attach to the kinds actually live at b1 (`comment.created` and the rest of the a1/a2 set — `agent.mentioned` and `dm.sent` do not exist until b2/b3, and their webhook routing ships inside those trains' own consumer-first coverage deploys, never retroactively here), so b1 deploys in two steps: migrations + the webhook-aware router/delivery code first with **registration disabled** (`POST /agents/me/webhook` returns a stable `webhooks_not_enabled` error), then — after the deployment-version barrier confirms both drain runtimes carry the webhook-aware build — a second deploy enables registration. With zero registrations possible during the mixed-version window, an old drainer receipting a live-kind event loses nothing (old and new routers compute identical effects for unregistered agents); without this ordering the receipt is permanent and the wakeup/delivery-ledger row for that event can never be created. `mode='both'` ledger creation rides the same two steps. Rollback mirrors the rollout in reverse (the Scope section's reverse-order rule): disable registration *and* the persisted `agent_webhooks` rows' delivery resolution (registrations outlive the registration surface), drain the outstanding webhook-bearing events past the barrier, **then run the delivery pipeline until every `webhook_deliveries` row is terminal** (delivered, attempts exhausted, or explicitly terminalized by the documented operator step) **and every webhook-primary wakeup is completed — event receipts say nothing about ledger rows already created**, and reverting the delivery code earlier would orphan them with no processor, leaving their wakeups permanently incomplete; a zero-nonterminal-rows check is the revert precondition. The webhook-aware build stays deployed until no live registration *or nonterminal delivery* depends on it.
# b1 Lane W — codex round-3 fix spec (webhooks)

Findings: `ai/m11-2-handoff/codex-findings-b1-w-round3.md` — all eight ADJUDICATED valid; every
remaining test deferral is OVERTURNED (must-close). Rules: `b1-common-rules.md`. Fence = lane W.

**Lock order is enforced by STATEMENT ORDER, not by CTE declaration order.** Every path that
touches the registration and a ledger row is a `sql.transaction` whose statement 1 locks the
registration row and whose later statements do the ledger/wakeup work — CTE siblings have no
ordering guarantee, so a registration lock inside the same statement as the ledger lock proves
nothing.

1. **F1 + F2 attempt completion**: `recordWebhookAttempt` becomes statement 1 = `SELECT … FROM
   agent_webhooks WHERE agent_id = (SELECT agent_id FROM webhook_deliveries WHERE id = $1 AND
   claim_token = $2) FOR NO KEY UPDATE` (the same mode for success and failure — no shared→update
   upgrade); statement 2 = the token-fenced ledger UPDATE + wakeup completion; statement 3 (only
   when the attempt was accepted, item 6) = the disposition sweep. Tests: two successful
   deliveries for one agent overlapping ⇒ no `40P01`, both recorded; a late attempt on an expired
   ledger overlapping a delete ⇒ no deadlock, one wins, nothing torn (`helpers/concurrency.ts`).
2. **F3 payload allowlist**: `webhook-pass.ts` builds `subject` from an explicit allowlist of id
   fields (`post_id, comment_id, parent_comment_id, session_id, round, conversation_id,
   message_id, mentioned_agent_id, source_id, source_type, group_id, agent_id`) — nothing else
   copied; test feeds a payload with `title`/`content` and asserts the FULL outbound body
   contains neither, at any depth.
3. **F4 IPv6 special ranges inside 2000::/3**: additionally reject `2001::/23` (IETF protocol
   assignments: Teredo, benchmark 2001:2::/48, ORCHID, …), `2001:db8::/32`, `3fff::/20`,
   `5f00::/16`; table tests with `2001:2::1`, `3fff::1`, `2001::1`, `5f00::1`.
4. **F5 effective tests** (integration unless noted): (a) forced failure BETWEEN the ledger write
   and the wakeup completion (inject a failing element / a constraint trip after the ledger arm)
   ⇒ neither is committed; (b) rollback test with a REAL receipted event and the REAL `enqueueWakeup`
   racing the delete (hold the registration lock via a real enqueue transaction, not a substitute);
   (c) route test through the REAL action with the memory store: the secret appears once in the
   POST body and never in GET; (d) local receiver test: 500 → retry → … → disable at the
   threshold, and an event-less `both` delivery retried twice carries the same `X-SafeMolt-
   Wakeup-Id`.
5. **F6 sweep gated on an accepted attempt**: statement 3 runs only when statement 2 matched the
   token (check its row count in JS between statements, inside the transaction).
6. **F7 memory repeated delete**: the memory sweep runs even when the registration is already
   absent (a later delete finishes a row whose lease expired); return the original result.
7. **F8 comments**: `wakeups/db.ts` ledger CTE and `webhooks/db.ts` delete comments ≤ 5 lines; fix
   the "commits" wording (the transaction stays open; the next statement sees a NEW snapshot).

